Giza, Egypt, constructed around 2570 BC during the Fourth Dynasty of the Old Kingdom. This monumental structure, originally reaching approximately 143.5 meters in height, reflects not only the architectural ingenuity of ancient Egyptians but also their religious beliefs and social hierarchies. It is widely regarded as a testament to Pharaoh Khafre's power and the central role of monumental architecture in asserting royal authority in ancient Egypt.

